   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "Let \\( y = h(x) \\) be a solution of the differential equation \\( \\frac{dy}{dx} = -(x - 1)(x - 4)(x - 9) \\), defined for \\( 2 < x < 10 \\). Find the value of \\( x \\) at which \\( h \\) has a relative maximum."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": 9
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "The differential equation gives the derivative directly: \\( h'(x) = -(x - 1)(x - 4)(x - 9) \\) for \\( 2 < x < 10 \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Multiply",
            [
               "Add",
               1,
               [
                  "Negate",
                  "x"
               ]
            ],
            [
               "Add",
               "x",
               -9
            ],
            [
               "Add",
               "x",
               -4
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Set \\( h'(x) = 0 \\) and factor: \\( -(x - 1)(x - 4)(x - 9) = 0 \\), so \\( x = 1, 4, 9 \\).",
         "mathjson": 9
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Only \\( x = 4 \\) and \\( x = 9 \\) lie in \\( 2 < x < 10 \\); \\( x = 1 \\) is outside the interval on which \\( h \\) is defined, so it is not a critical point of \\( h \\).",
         "mathjson": 9
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "\\( h' \\) changes from positive to negative at \\( x = 9 \\) and from negative to positive at \\( x = 4 \\), so \\( h \\) has its relative maximum at \\( x = 9 \\).",
         "mathjson": 9
      }
   ],
